 It's pretty incredible.

51
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:56,060
 Hey, I've really loved what God has been doing here

52
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:57,980
 these last few weeks.

53
00:01:57,980 --> 00:01:59,740
 We were just visiting as a team in the back

54
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:01,780
 and we were talking about what a privilege it

55
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:03,940
 is for us to get to step into these Tuesday nights

56
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:06,020
 and be a part of what God is doing.

57
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:09,940
 You see, there can be this temptation in big ministry

58
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:13,020
 spheres like the one we're sitting in tonight.

59
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:15,300
 Just think that we're here and we're just

60
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:16,660
 executing a service.

61
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:19,980
 And yet, I just hope you know from my heart, our team's heart,

62
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:23,500
 we're not interested in executing a service.

63
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:28,220
 We're very interested instead in acting as servants.

64
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,420
 That's what we believe God has asked us to do.

65
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:33,700
 We think that our greatest service to you

66
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:37,300
 is worshiping in truth and opening God's word

67
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:40,620
 and telling you what He has to say as opposed to anything

68
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:41,380
 that I think.

69
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:44,460
 And so I am really excited to get into the text with you,

70
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:46,260
 but I think it would be helpful for me just

71
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:50,300
 to set up the evening with a quick story.

72
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,220
 One of the unique occupational hazards

73
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:53,980
 of being a young adult pastor is that I

74
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:58,340
 get the privilege of journeying with engaged couples

75
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:00,380
 between their engagement to their wedding day.

76
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:02,140
 I get to do a lot of counseling.

77
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:04,780
 And yet, what happens over the course of my counseling

78
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:08,220
 young adults in engagement is a really interesting phenomenon.

79
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:11,460
 Because before people get engaged, what we realize

80
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:17,660
 is oftentimes they're willing to take it slow, keep their pace,

81
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:22,500
 be prayerful, discerning, deliberate, and take months,

82
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:26,900
 if not years, and I mean years, to finally put a ring on it.

83
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,220
 They're anything but in a rush.

84
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:33,660
 But the minute they put that ring on it, everything changes.

85
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:35,620
 Like no longer are they trying just

86
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,380
 to move casually through the romantic landscape.

87
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:42,700
 Instead, they are in a rush to get to the altar.

88
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,900
 They know, Kylan, that wedding, it's in 170 days,

89
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:47,020
 four hours, and 12 minutes.

90
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:48,700
 I've got to get there as soon as I can.

91
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:51,700
 The change is so dramatic.

92
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:54,060
 Before, they've got Pinterest boards

93
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:59,020
 with like 437 different photos and just

94
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:00,980
 slides and slides and slides of things

95
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:02,980
 that they want to be true, like centerpieces

96
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:04,260
 and floral arrangements.

97
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:07,020
 And they've already selected their color palette.

98
00:04:07,020 --> 00:04:08,820
 They want dusty sunset sage.

99
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:11,820
 But the minute they decide to get married,

100
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,620
 they're looking at their Pinterest

101
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:15,860
 and they're thinking, we ain't got time for that.

102
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:17,780
 You think the courthouse is open on Tuesday?

103
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:20,060
 Because they're in a rush to go ahead

104
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:22,980
 and seal the deal before the engagement.

105
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:27,020
 I walk with couples that have so very intentionally,

106
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:30,940
 purposefully pursued purity with one another.

107
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:32,900
 And yet when they get engaged,

108
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,940
 it's like I'm trying to keep two magnets off of each other.

109
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:38,660
 They just want to touch constantly.

110
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:40,860
 This was what happens whenever people go

111
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:43,380
 from singleness to engagement.

112
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:46,260
 They find themselves in a moment where,

113
00:04:46,260 --> 00:04:48,940
 because they know what's coming,

114
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:53,300
 waiting is the hardest thing to do in the world.

115
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:54,700
 Now, why do I tell you that?

116
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:58,620
 Because this is similar to our experience.

117
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:01,500
 You see, what engaged couples experience

118
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:04,100
 is they don't experience difficulty

119
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:06,100
 through the uncertainty of what they want.

120
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:07,660
 They definitely know what they want.

121
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,020
 What they're experiencing is the difficulty

122
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:12,020
 of inactivity instead.

123
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:14,420
 They're stuck in a holding pattern.

124
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:16,260
 They just want to get a move on.

125
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:18,620
 And for many of us, we know what that's like,

126
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:22,620
 because when we have clarity of what we want in life,

127
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:26,700
 yet we cannot gain traction in pursuit of that thing.

128
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:29,540
 And we find ourselves waiting on God

129
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:32,340
 to move us towards our destination.

130
00:05:32,340 --> 00:05:34,740
 We can grow and see ourselves.

131
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:36,260
 I don't know what it is you're waiting for.

132
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:38,820
 I don't know if you're waiting to be married yourself,

133
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:40,660
 if you're waiting for the right job to come along

134
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:42,340
 to finally land that promotion,

135
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:44,380
 if you're waiting for that family member,

136
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:47,060
 that friend of yours to actually get well,

137
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:48,860
 if you're waiting for clarity

138
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:51,020
 of what God's purpose in your life is,

139
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:54,340
 if you're waiting for your pain to finally subside,

140
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:57,220
 whatever it is, it's easy to know what you want,

141
00:05:57,220 --> 00:05:58,780
 but it is a much more difficult thing

142
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:00,060
 to actually wait for it.

143
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:04,420
 Which is why society has become so proficient

144
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:06,660
 at eliminating the need for waiting.

145
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:10,700
 Historically, humans spent most of their lives

146
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:13,340
 waiting within environments

147
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:15,140
 because rewards were often delayed.

148
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:18,380
 Like crops took months to actually grow,

149
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:21,740
 travel took days in the car as a family,

150
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:24,500
 entertainment actually had to be planned.

151
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,500
 And if you wanted to get out there

152
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:32,340
 and actually like find information,

153
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:33,420
 if you were doing research,

154
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:35,340
 you would have to consult with an expert,

155
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:38,020
 read through the paper, go to the public library.

156
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:39,460
 Do we even still have those?

157
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:40,460
 This is what you would have to do

158
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:43,420
 if you were trying to gather information for yourself.

159
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:44,980
 For all of human history,

160
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:48,140
 waiting was a normal part of your experience,

161
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:51,820
 but such is not the case any longer.

162
00:06:51,820 --> 00:06:55,500
 We don't live in a patient culture.

163
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:59,300
 We live in an instant culture.

164
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:04,300
 And what's crazy is it has happened within our generation.

165
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:09,300
 Like before, you would wait weeks for a letter to arrive.

166
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:11,260
 And yet now you're wondering,

167
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:12,500
 are those three gray dots

168
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:15,240
 ever actually going to turn into a message?

169
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:19,100
 Before, you would wait what felt like forever

170
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:20,140
 for photos to develop.

171
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:23,500
 And yet now you delete something like 47 selfies

172
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:25,620
 before you walk out of the parking lot.

173
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:28,620
 Before, you would wait for the family computer to be free.

174
00:07:28,620 --> 00:07:30,340
 Anybody ever been in that spot?

175
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:32,920
 And yet now you get so annoyed

176
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:34,980
 if your Safari doesn't load a screen

177
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:36,260
 within the first three seconds.

178
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:38,980
 Before, you would wait all year

179
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:41,260
 for your favorite movie to come out.

180
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:45,220
 And yet today we get so frustrated

181
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:48,100
 if we are on the front page of Netflix

182
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:49,180
 and we're scrolling along

183
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:50,300
 and we can't find anything

184
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:52,660
 that actually engages our interest.

185
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:54,700
 Before, you would wait by the radio

186
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:56,580
 in hopes that they would play your favorite song.

187
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:58,420
 I remember doing this.

188
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:01,920
 And yet now you're so sick of that favorite song

189
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,300
 because you've listened to it over and over and over again.

190
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:05,940
 You're burnt out.

191
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:08,940
 Before, you would wait whenever a friend told you

192
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:10,300
 they would come by to pick you up.

193
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:11,980
 You would wait for them to do so.

194
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:13,860
 And yet now you can just track their location

195
00:08:13,860 --> 00:08:15,620
 and then complain that it took them so long

196
00:08:15,620 --> 00:08:18,020
 to get to your place.

197
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:21,980
 Before, you would wait days if not weeks

198
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:23,400
 for a package to arrive.

199
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:24,580
 And yet just yesterday,

200
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:26,860
 I saw Amazon dropping one off to my neighbor

201
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:29,460
 by way of drone after just a couple of hours

202
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:31,720
 of actually having made the purchase.

203
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:34,260
 This is the great irony we're facing today.

204
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:37,140
 Things are more convenient than ever.

205
00:08:37,140 --> 00:08:41,160
 And yet we're more impatient than ever.

206
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:46,780
 And the reason for that is because

207
00:08:46,780 --> 00:08:49,700
 we either have not learned

208
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:54,660
 or we have forgotten the secret to waiting well.

209
00:08:54,660 --> 00:08:57,820
 And so tonight I wanna talk to you

210
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:00,480
 about what it actually means to wait well

211
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:03,320
 whenever God seemingly has you in a holding pattern.

212
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:04,520
 So if you have a Bible,

213
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:07,840
 turn with me to the book of Lamentations chapter three.

214
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:11,760
 While you're figuring out where that book is,

215
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:13,380
 let me just say this.

216
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:18,660
 The goal of Porch 20 has been to invite voices

217
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:20,140
 that have been influential over the course

218
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:21,480
 of the Porch's history.

219
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:23,980
 And our prompt to the guys that have been here,

220
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:26,300
 JP and JD and DeMar,

221
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:28,300
 was to have them teach a lesson

222
00:09:28,300 --> 00:09:30,100
 that they felt like God had taught them

223
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:30,940
 through their time.

224
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:33,580
 Well, I've been here just short of three years.

225
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:35,220
 I have not had near the amount of time

226
00:09:35,220 --> 00:09:38,340
 that those guys have had by way of their experience

227
00:09:38,340 --> 00:09:39,180
 at the Porch.

228
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:43,220
 And yet the lesson that comes most easily to me,

229
00:09:43,220 --> 00:09:45,180
 that which springs first to mind

230
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:47,820
 when I consider my time at the Porch

231
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:50,840
 is what it means to wait well.

232
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:52,520
 The reason why is because

233
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:55,560
 if you haven't picked up on it already,

234
00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:57,460
 if you haven't heard me say it from this stage,

235
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:00,820
 I'm just not interested in playing the Christian game.

236
00:10:00,820 --> 00:10:02,720
 Like it would be so easy to roll in

237
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,220
 and just pump a lot of smoke

238
00:10:05,220 --> 00:10:07,140
 and raise the volume to 11

239
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:09,020
 and make sure the lights are dizzying

240
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:10,560
 and have incredible worship

241
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:13,020
 and make sure the teaching is relevant yet biblical

242
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:16,900
 to do all of the right industry standard plays

243
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:18,420
 to make sure we get butts in seats.

244
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:20,800
 But I'm just not concerned with that.

245
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:24,280
 Like I'm not worried about having a really,

246
00:10:26,140 --> 00:10:29,240
 a really popular ministry.

247
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:33,580
 I'm more concerned with having a very powerful ministry.

248
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:35,580
 And yet what I know is there's no power found in me

249
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:36,780
 nor in us.

250
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:39,720
 There's only power in the name of Jesus.

251
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:43,400
 And yet, if you're going to lean into the power of Christ,

252
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:44,740
 you have to go where he wants,

253
00:10:44,740 --> 00:10:47,100
 you have to move when he wants,

254
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:49,460
 which requires a lot of waiting.

255
00:10:49,460 --> 00:10:51,460
 There are moments where we're asking him to do things

256
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:54,100
 and yet he has not given us clearance to do it.

257
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:56,620
 There are moments where we're pleading and praying

258
00:10:56,620 --> 00:10:59,300
 and yet he has not provided us an answer.

259
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:01,220
 We have spent a lot of time waiting

260
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:04,440
 because waiting often feels like the most faithful response.

261
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:07,140
 Sure, we could do a lot through our giftedness,

262
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:08,540
 but I'm not concerned with that.

263
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:11,400
 I want to do things through God's greatness,

264
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:13,460
 but often we have to wait for those things.

265
00:11:13,460 --> 00:11:15,380
 And so this is the lesson that I've learned.

266
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:18,740
 And this text has been especially helpful for me

267
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:20,100
 as I've learned it.

268
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:22,820
 You see, just some context on the book of Lamentations.

269
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:25,340
 Lamentations is a book you don't normally

270
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:27,260
 find yourself studying

271
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:28,740
 because it's anything but lighthearted.

272
00:11:28,740 --> 00:11:33,740
 It is oft considered the saddest book in the entire Bible.

273
00:11:33,740 --> 00:11:36,580
 Most scholars agree that the prophet Jeremiah

274
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:37,420
 would have written it.

275
00:11:37,420 --> 00:11:38,740
 There's a little bit of debate on that.

276
00:11:38,740 --> 00:11:40,700
 And yet I'm convinced that he did.

277
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:43,140
 And yet what we know is that he wrote this book

278
00:11:43,140 --> 00:11:46,340
 in the days following Israel's darkest moments,

279
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:48,980
 following the Babylonian siege of Jerusalem.

280
00:11:49,820 --> 00:11:53,340
 The siege lasted two and a half years.

281
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:56,820
 In Babylon, they held the city of Jerusalem in a chokehold.

282
00:11:56,820 --> 00:12:00,960
 They starved its inhabitants to the point of driving them

283
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:05,580
 to cannibalistic behavior, enslaving one another,

284
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:07,780
 murdering even its own leaders.

285
00:12:07,780 --> 00:12:10,300
 It was a time of disorienting psychological,

286
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:13,860
 emotional, and spiritual distress for the people.

287
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:17,100
 And the whole while through, they were wondering

288
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:19,180
 what many of us wonder.

289
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:22,300
 What is taking you so long, God?

290
00:12:22,300 --> 00:12:25,700
 Why are we still waiting in this situation?

291
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:26,940
 Do you not hear me?

292
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:28,140
 Do you not see me?

293
00:12:28,140 --> 00:12:29,660
 Do you not care about me?

294
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:31,740
 When will you show up and deliver us

295
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:34,060
 from this present persecution?

296
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:41,540
 They knew what we know, that God is good,

297
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:43,980
 that God is sovereign, meaning he's in control,

298
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:46,420
 and that God has made and will keep his promises.

299
00:12:46,420 --> 00:12:49,020
 And yet they can't help but wonder what many of us,

300
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:52,340
 all across this room, are wondering,

301
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:57,340
 why am I waiting, and how am I supposed to do it if I must?

302
00:12:57,340 --> 00:13:01,380
 Well, Jeremiah's gonna tell us.

303
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:03,100
 He's gonna teach us the secret to waiting well.

304
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:07,460
 He starts teaching this lesson in verse 19.

305
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:13,860
 It says this, verse 19.

306
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:21,180
 "I remember my affliction and my wanderings,

307
00:13:21,180 --> 00:13:24,340
 "the wormwood and the gall.

308
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:27,460
 "My soul, it continually remembers it,

309
00:13:27,460 --> 00:13:30,060
 "and it's bowed down within me."

310
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:31,220
 So we'll stop there.

311
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:34,820
 Because what we need to realize is that Jeremiah

312
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:37,940
 is very clearly wrestling with his circumstances.

313
00:13:37,940 --> 00:13:39,140
 Like, I love it.

314
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:41,340
 He doesn't do what many Christians do today.

315
00:13:41,340 --> 00:13:42,820
 He doesn't put on a pretty face.

316
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:47,220
 He doesn't try to mask his difficulty.

317
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:48,980
 He just comes honest to God.

318
00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:50,900
 Like, he just approaches,

319
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:52,980
 and he doesn't pretend that everything is fine.

320
00:13:52,980 --> 00:13:55,620
 He doesn't put on a strong face and carry on.

321
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:58,060
 He doesn't act like everything is okay.

322
00:13:58,060 --> 00:14:02,540
 Instead, he knows that biblical, legitimate faith

323
00:14:02,540 --> 00:14:04,320
 functions in such a way

324
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:08,100
 that we bring our honest lament to God.

325
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:09,700
 He's not fake about it.

326
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:10,580
 He's real about it.

327
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:12,220
 You've heard me say from this stage,

328
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:14,020
 before you ever consider praying the right prayers,

329
00:14:14,020 --> 00:14:16,020
 you should pray the real prayers.

330
00:14:16,020 --> 00:14:19,180
 Because God wants the real you.

331
00:14:19,180 --> 00:14:20,180
 And so that's what he does.

332
00:14:20,180 --> 00:14:22,100
 He comes honest to the Lord.

333
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:24,280
 He knows that my faith does not deny

334
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:25,900
 the existence of my pain,

335
00:14:25,900 --> 00:14:29,580
 and yet my faith relies on God through the pain.

336
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:32,700
 He gets honest.

337
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:36,260
 And as he does, we see that he begins to express

338
00:14:36,260 --> 00:14:40,020
 three effects of the cause of his waiting,

339
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:42,540
 three things that result in his life

340
00:14:42,540 --> 00:14:46,100
 as he has wandered through this window of waiting

341
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:47,920
 amidst their Babylonian siege.

342
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:51,020
 The first thing is he tells us that he's discouraged.

343
00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:54,300
 That's what the word affliction means in verse 19.

344
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:56,880
 The Hebrew idea there of being afflicted

345
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,540
 is that he's carrying a heavy load.

346
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:02,100
 Like, he's weighed down.

347
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:06,980
 He's bowed before as a result of some burden in his life.

348
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:08,800
 He's discouraged.

349
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:10,540
 What he does is he describes waiting

350
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:13,500
 like carrying a weighted vest.

351
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:15,580
 Anybody ruck in here?

352
00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:17,380
 Go for ruck walks?

353
00:15:17,380 --> 00:15:19,220
 I'm a young adult dad.

354
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:21,700
 It's like the thing that dads are doing nowadays.

355
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:22,900
 They put on these weighted vests

356
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:24,740
 and they kind of power walk the neighborhood.

357
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:26,740
 And what you find is that the weighted vests

358
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:28,500
 are light to begin with,

359
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:31,540
 but quickly they take what is a casual stroll

360
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:34,340
 and they turn it into a total body workout.

361
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:37,300
 Because that's what weight does in your life.

362
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:39,620
 Like, it exhausts you.

363
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:44,620
 In the same way, waiting will emotionally fatigue you.

364
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:46,140
 It will zap you of your strength.

365
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:49,260
 It will suck the life, the joy out of you.

366
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:51,460
 He's discouraged, afflicted.

367
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:54,980
 The second effect that we see is he says

368
00:15:54,980 --> 00:15:59,500
 that he's been wandering, which means he's restless.

369
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:01,260
 That's what you do when you wander.

370
00:16:01,260 --> 00:16:03,180
 Like, when you find yourself in a season or situation

371
00:16:03,180 --> 00:16:04,900
 that you don't wanna be in, many of us,

372
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:07,060
 we don't wait on God to get us out of it.

373
00:16:07,060 --> 00:16:10,100
 We just take action ourselves.

374
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:12,700
 We start wandering around.

375
00:16:12,700 --> 00:16:15,100
 We see this, at least I see this,

376
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:17,700
 probably most frequently within romantic relationships.

377
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:19,780
 People will lower their standards

378
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:21,500
 and they will wander towards people

379
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:24,420
 that they should not have any interest in dating.

380
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:26,660
 And yet they will do so, why?

381
00:16:26,660 --> 00:16:29,980
 Because they're sick of being single.

382
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:32,420
 Like, they just wanna get out of that situation

383
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:34,580
 and they think God has promised them they will.

384
00:16:34,580 --> 00:16:36,700
 And yet because he's taking too long,

385
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:39,740
 they start to take action themselves.

386
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:43,220
 And what happens is they wander where they don't belong.

387
00:16:43,220 --> 00:16:45,100
 Some of you, you've done this with your career.

388
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:46,820
 Like, you've started applying for jobs

389
00:16:46,820 --> 00:16:48,780
 that you have no interest in,

390
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:50,740
 nor are you actually qualified for.

391
00:16:50,740 --> 00:16:54,100
 And the reason you're doing it is 'cause

392
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:55,940
 you just wanna change.

393
00:16:55,940 --> 00:16:59,420
 Like, you just want out of the very thing that you've,

394
00:16:59,420 --> 00:17:02,700
 you've went out of the very job that you have.

395
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:05,180
 And yet because you're tired of waiting on God

396
00:17:05,180 --> 00:17:06,660
 to make the difference,

397
00:17:06,660 --> 00:17:07,900
 you're willing to make it yourself.

398
00:17:07,900 --> 00:17:11,140
 And what it's done is it has led you to wander into careers

399
00:17:11,140 --> 00:17:16,620
 that you have no interest, nor business, being a part of.

400
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:19,340
 I could go on, but this is what happens when we wander.

401
00:17:19,340 --> 00:17:22,220
 And then the third thing that we know is,

402
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:23,900
 is the effect of waiting in Jeremiah,

403
00:17:23,900 --> 00:17:26,820
 as well as our lives, is it makes us bitter.

404
00:17:26,820 --> 00:17:30,820
 That's what the two words, wormwood and gall, actually mean.

405
00:17:30,820 --> 00:17:33,060
 They're references to bitter herbs.

406
00:17:33,060 --> 00:17:37,980
 And so waiting does to us what waiting does to food.

407
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:40,740
 It makes us sour, spoiled, bitter.

408
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:45,360
 Bitter towards God, bitter towards others,

409
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:48,900
 and even bitter towards ourselves.

410
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:50,900
 Waiting is consequential.

411
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:54,340
 It has an impressive and impactful impact

412
00:17:54,340 --> 00:17:56,380
 in the life of a person.

413
00:17:56,380 --> 00:17:58,500
 It was his experience, it's your experience

414
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:00,740
 for many of you, even right now here tonight.

415
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:04,500
 Like as you look back on your own season of waiting,

416
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:07,520
 maybe for some of you, it's for him to propose,

417
00:18:07,520 --> 00:18:10,100
 it's for that recruiter to finally get back to you,

418
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,060
 it's for work to finally let up,

419
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:15,260
 it's for test results to at last come in.

420
00:18:15,260 --> 00:18:16,880
 I don't know what it is for you.

421
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:19,620
 Maybe it's a break from your chronic pain.

422
00:18:19,620 --> 00:18:22,060
 And yet as you're waiting for all of these things,

423
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:23,980
 you do what he does.

424
00:18:23,980 --> 00:18:27,540
 You grow exhausted.

425
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:33,380
 You begin to wander around,

426
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:36,780
 turning towards different things to help you cope

427
00:18:36,780 --> 00:18:38,640
 in the face of your pain,

428
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:41,440
 and you start feeling less like yourself

429
00:18:41,440 --> 00:18:42,840
 and more like someone else.

430
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:46,900
 But there's hope.

431
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:49,780
 Because he says this in verse 21.

432
00:18:49,780 --> 00:18:52,380
 He says, "But this I call to mind,

433
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:54,220
 "and therefore I have hope."

434
00:18:54,220 --> 00:18:56,560
 We'll stop there, which is a terrible place

435
00:18:56,560 --> 00:18:59,540
 to stop admittedly, because he's going to tell you

436
00:18:59,540 --> 00:19:01,260
 what gives him hope.

437
00:19:01,260 --> 00:19:03,860
 But before we talk about what actually gives Jeremiah hope

438
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,980
 in this moment, and can also give you hope in your waiting,

439
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:08,300
 we need to acknowledge something important

440
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:10,140
 that just happened here, because verse 21

441
00:19:10,140 --> 00:19:15,140
 is the turning point of this entire section of Scripture.

442
00:19:15,140 --> 00:19:17,620
 What just happened was a mental shift.

443
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:20,500
 He went from remembering twice, it says he remembered.

444
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:21,700
 He remembered and he remembered,

445
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:24,700
 and yet, "This I have called to mind."

446
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:27,060
 He changes his perspective.

447
00:19:27,060 --> 00:19:29,660
 He stops remembering his pain,

448
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:32,060
 and he starts recalling God's truth,

449
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,060
 because he knows, and this is the first thing

450
00:19:34,060 --> 00:19:36,700
 you gotta get if you wanna know the secret to waiting well,

451
00:19:36,700 --> 00:19:40,420
 is that whatever fills your mind will fill your heart.

452
00:19:40,420 --> 00:19:43,740
 Whatever fills your mind will fill your heart.

453
00:19:43,740 --> 00:19:44,980
 That's the first secret.

454
00:19:44,980 --> 00:19:46,580
 Like, we need to know.

455
00:19:46,580 --> 00:19:49,540
 We are in control of our thoughts.

456
00:19:49,540 --> 00:19:50,880
 That's why Paul says it like this

457
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:52,900
 in his letter to the church of Philippi.

458
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:56,660
 He says, "Finally, brothers, whatever's true,

459
00:19:56,660 --> 00:19:58,900
 "or honorable, or just, whatever's pure,

460
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:00,640
 "whatever's lovely, whatever's commendable,

461
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:02,640
 "if there is any excellence,

462
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:05,140
 "if there is anything worthy of praise,

463
00:20:05,140 --> 00:20:08,420
 "think about these things, not the other things,

464
00:20:08,420 --> 00:20:10,580
 "not the less than excellent things,

465
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:12,140
 "not the things of this world.

466
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:14,740
 "Think about those things which are of God."

467
00:20:14,740 --> 00:20:16,220
 And yet, he goes on and he says,

468
00:20:16,220 --> 00:20:19,820
 "Because whatever you've learned, or received,

469
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:22,060
 "or heard from me, or seen in me,

470
00:20:22,060 --> 00:20:25,900
 "all of those things describe a conceptualization,

471
00:20:25,900 --> 00:20:29,140
 "an understanding in their mind

472
00:20:29,140 --> 00:20:31,140
 "of what Paul has taught them."

473
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:34,060
 He then says, "Put it into practice."

474
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:38,380
 Because he knows your thoughts

475
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:41,660
 directly influence your actions.

476
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:44,580
 Like, what goes into your mind

477
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:47,860
 has a way of making its way down into your heart,

478
00:20:47,860 --> 00:20:49,820
 and has a way of ultimately making its way

479
00:20:49,820 --> 00:20:52,260
 out through your hands.

480
00:20:52,260 --> 00:20:54,180
 I remember experiencing this

481
00:20:54,180 --> 00:20:56,780
 when I had my very first season of fantasy football.

482
00:20:56,780 --> 00:20:59,900
 I'd never played before,

483
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:02,340
 and yet, something that I know about myself

484
00:21:02,340 --> 00:21:05,180
 is I have an extremely obsessive personality.

485
00:21:05,180 --> 00:21:06,460
 I have to be careful,

486
00:21:06,460 --> 00:21:08,700
 because I'm not a mile-wide, inch-deep kind of guy.

487
00:21:08,700 --> 00:21:10,180
 I've got a few interests,

488
00:21:10,180 --> 00:21:12,820
 and yet, I'm very invested in those things.

489
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:15,740
 And so, if I was gonna crack the door on fantasy football,

490
00:21:15,740 --> 00:21:17,500
 I had to know that I was entering

491
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:19,860
 into a long-term relationship with it.

492
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:24,860
 So when I did, I became deeply interested,

493
00:21:24,860 --> 00:21:28,420
 because I am filling my mind

494
00:21:28,420 --> 00:21:30,940
 with thoughts of all these different players,

495
00:21:30,940 --> 00:21:33,500
 none of whom I have ever cared about in the past.

496
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:37,060
 And yet, I now care about them deeply,

497
00:21:37,060 --> 00:21:40,740
 because those thoughts have made their way into my heart.

498
00:21:40,740 --> 00:21:44,060
 And I care so much that I'm giving up hours on Sundays

499
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:48,060
 watching games that don't have any geographic relevance

500
00:21:48,060 --> 00:21:49,740
 to me whatsoever.

501
00:21:49,740 --> 00:21:50,780
 Games that have required me

502
00:21:50,780 --> 00:21:52,860
 to download alternative streaming sources,

503
00:21:52,860 --> 00:21:56,500
 so that way I can check in and see how the Browns are doing,

504
00:21:56,500 --> 00:21:58,060
 which is never very well.

505
00:21:58,060 --> 00:22:00,180
 (audience laughing)

506
00:22:00,180 --> 00:22:03,820
 And it moved me from a place of just caring

507
00:22:03,820 --> 00:22:06,020
 to actually acting on it,

508
00:22:06,020 --> 00:22:09,780
 because through the week, from Sunday to Sunday,

509
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:14,780
 I would track stats, I would listen to podcasts,

510
00:22:14,780 --> 00:22:15,820
 I would neglect my family.

511
00:22:15,820 --> 00:22:17,580
 I'm just kidding, I'm not neglecting my family

512
00:22:17,580 --> 00:22:18,660
 for fantasy football.

513
00:22:19,660 --> 00:22:22,620
 My thoughts directly influenced my actions.

514
00:22:22,620 --> 00:22:23,460
 Do you see how that works?

515
00:22:23,460 --> 00:22:24,620
 This is the way it works for everyone.

516
00:22:24,620 --> 00:22:28,500
 Ben Stewart, one of my greatest influences and dear friends,

517
00:22:28,500 --> 00:22:30,380
 he said, "What you think about determines

518
00:22:30,380 --> 00:22:31,220
 "what you care about.

519
00:22:31,220 --> 00:22:33,140
 "What you care about determines what you chase."

520
00:22:33,140 --> 00:22:34,780
 That's the way that this works.

521
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:36,900
 So if you're thinking to yourself,

522
00:22:36,900 --> 00:22:40,100
 "Ah, man, they probably don't like me,"

523
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:41,500
 then guess what will happen?

524
00:22:41,500 --> 00:22:46,260
 You will start avoiding eye contact,

525
00:22:46,260 --> 00:22:49,180
 you'll stop speaking in public,

526
00:22:49,180 --> 00:22:51,100
 and you will find as soon as Irish goodbye,

527
00:22:51,100 --> 00:22:53,540
 you can to get out of that situation.

528
00:22:53,540 --> 00:22:57,780
 If you think to yourself, "This thing has to be perfect,"

529
00:22:57,780 --> 00:23:00,460
 more than likely you're going to procrastinate

530
00:23:00,460 --> 00:23:02,420
 ever beginning that thing,

531
00:23:02,420 --> 00:23:06,420
 because the impossibility of giving it

532
00:23:06,420 --> 00:23:07,980
 everything it deserves will keep you

533
00:23:07,980 --> 00:23:10,420
 from ever actually starting the work itself.

534
00:23:11,820 --> 00:23:16,700
 If you find yourself thinking, "That sounded so weird,

535
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:19,020
 "why did I text that?"

536
00:23:19,020 --> 00:23:20,900
 You will inevitably reread that text message

537
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:23,100
 like 18, 19 times,

538
00:23:23,100 --> 00:23:25,820
 trying to diagnose how not to do that on the next text,

539
00:23:25,820 --> 00:23:28,140
 and yet you'll never actually send the next text.

540
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:29,900
 You see, our thoughts drive our actions.

541
00:23:29,900 --> 00:23:31,660
 This is why it is your responsibility

542
00:23:31,660 --> 00:23:33,060
 to manage your own thoughts.

543
00:23:33,060 --> 00:23:34,660
 It's not anyone else's responsibility,

544
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:36,500
 it is your responsibility.

545
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:39,020
 And we know from the scripture,

546
00:23:39,020 --> 00:23:40,780
 you take every thought captive.

547
00:23:40,780 --> 00:23:44,780
 You have the ability, the authority,

548
00:23:44,780 --> 00:23:46,540
 to consider what enters into your mind

549
00:23:46,540 --> 00:23:50,100
 and whether or not it actually has a place there.

550
00:23:50,100 --> 00:23:51,140
 So let me ask you,

551
00:23:51,140 --> 00:23:53,740
 Porch, what are you thinking about

552
00:23:53,740 --> 00:23:56,900
 whenever you find yourself in a window of waiting?

553
00:23:56,900 --> 00:23:58,300
 What are you thinking about?

554
00:23:58,300 --> 00:24:01,540
 Whenever life is complicated

555
00:24:01,540 --> 00:24:03,020
 and your dreams are out there,

556
00:24:03,020 --> 00:24:04,220
 but you're stuck back here,

